in the battle against coronavirus this  is the body armor but shortages mean  medics are being asked to reuse some of  their disposable protective gear public  health England is even recommending  plastic aprons  if gowns are unavailable one NHS worker  at an intensive care unit in London told  us of his concerns as stocks run out he  wanted to remain anonymous you can't say  to people for this amount of time these  gowns are disposable and then the next  day say Oh actually we've changed our  mind that's the difficulty if they'd  said from the very beginning we can  safely reuse equipments then it wouldn't  be a problem the problem is that it is  constantly changing because it is very  much reliant on the stock not the  evidence as well as suspicions there's  anger at perceived failings in the  supply chain and its impact on the NHS  frontline staff will have to make  difficult decisions awful decisions  about whether they are able to continue  caring the new advice gives us if you  like you know bronze-level  adequacy and second best in this  situation is just not good enough  ministers and officials accept it's a  challenge but they're facing criticism  more isn't being done to source  equipment from local suppliers why is  there such a national shortage is it a  failure to to stockpile in the first  place is it a failure of the supply  chain to buy and distribute efficiently  the government does stockpile PPE for  pandemics and that has been incredibly  important and useful in our response to  this but as you hinted at this is a  global crisis a global emergency and  there is undoubtedly a strain on supply  chains for PPE the demand for certain  items of personal protective equipment  has increased 5,000 percent since the  pandemic started this week a plane load  of supplies arrived in the UK  from Shanghai the gravest shortage is in  clinical gowns and China is the only  immediate bulk source but there's  massive global competition for  protective gear and it's causing delays  with orders once stock arrives in the UK  it must pass a safety test something  that's not always guaranteed they were  ordered a month ago that the  consignments are coming in fits and  starts and sometimes when they are  arriving they are either not what was  ordered by e their masks instead of  gowns for example or they aren't passing  the stringent quality tests that are  required in this country china isn't the  only answer turkey too has airlifted  supplies and will deliver more tomorrow  including 400,000 gowns  it's the equivalent of what the NHS goes  through in two to three days  just-in-time PPE not a long-term  solution  Deborah Haynes Sky News
 

